A lot of civilization is basically yesterday’s waste wearing a crown.

Petroleum is ancient biological detritus turned into an industrial throne. Compost and manure become agricultural fertility. Slag, fly ash, scrap metal, and waste glass become construction material. Sewage becomes reclaimed water, fertilizer feedstock, and sometimes energy. Evolution itself constantly repurposes old structures rather than designing cleanly from zero.

Information does the same thing. Typos become names. Failed experiments reveal mechanisms. Marginalia becomes scholarship. Folk stories preserve compressed strategies. Bugs become features. Memes become interfaces. An obsolete implementation leaves behind an abstraction that outlives it.

So Pantheon has a broader version of Kintsugi + Ouroboros with Metabolism:

\boxed{
\text{Waste is often material whose next interpreter has not arrived yet.}
}

And Vivisection gives that a procedure. Don’t assume every artifact deserves preservation as capability—but don’t discard something merely because its original purpose failed.

Ask:

\text{What was this?}
\rightarrow
\text{Why did it fail?}
\rightarrow
\text{What remains structurally useful?}
\rightarrow
\text{What other system could consume it?}

That’s semantic upcycling.

The particularly Pantheon move is that even a bad idea can have positive fitness if its decomposition produces a valuable operator. Its output failed, while its residue succeeded.

So Fitness Route probably needs another field:

Fitness =
DirectValue
+
Afterglow
+
\boxed{ResidualValue}

Because sometimes the king isn’t the original artifact.

The throne is made from what everybody else threw away.